<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mikulov</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Nikolsburg</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Mikulov, German Nikolsburg, is a town in South <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moravia">Moravia</a> near the Austrian border, known for its castle, its vineyards and its historic Jewish community, long the seat of the chief rabbi of Moravia. The castle was held by the Dietrichstein family from 1575. In July 1866 the preliminary peace ending the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austro-prussian-war">Austro-Prussian War</a> was signed there.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mikulov.jpg" alt="Mikulov" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Juhele_CZ · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:2019-11-06_13-04-11_Pentax_JH.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Mikulov is a town of about 7,600 inhabitants in Břeclav District, South Moravia, on the border with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>, about 18 km north-west of Břeclav and 42 km south of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brno">Brno</a>. Most of its territory lies in the Pálava Protected Landscape Area. The name comes from Mikul, a short form of Mikuláš, the Czech form of Nicholas; the German name Nikolsburg prevailed in the earliest times, and Mikulov has been used since the 19th century.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>A deed mentioning Mikulov in 1173 is a hoax of the 14th century. The first trustworthy mention is from 1249, when the Přemyslid margrave Ottokar II granted a castle and its surroundings to the Austrian noble Henry I of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liechtenstein">Liechtenstein</a>. In 1572 Emperor Maximilian II granted the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fief">fief</a> to Adam of Dietrichstein, and from 1575 until the 20th century the town belonged to the Dietrichstein family and its Mensdorff-Pouilly successors. Under Cardinal Franz von Dietrichstein it became one of the most important towns in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moravia">Moravia</a>, and in 1621 he signed the Treaty of Nikolsburg with the Transylvanian prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gabriel-bethlen">Gabriel Bethlen</a> at the castle. After a fire in 1719 the family rebuilt the castle to its present appearance.</p><p>Jewish settlement began in 1421, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albert-ii-duke-of-austria">Albert II of Austria</a> expelled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-austria">Lower Austria</a>, and more came after 1454. In the first half of the 16th century Nikolsburg became the seat of the regional rabbi of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moravia">Moravia</a>. Between 1553 and 1573 Judah Loew ben Bezalel officiated there for twenty years. A <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> of 1754 counted some 620 Jewish families, about 3,000 people and half the town. In 1938 there were 472 Jewish residents. Only 110 emigrated in time, and 327 did not survive the Holocaust. The German population, 98 per cent in 1890 and 82 per cent in 1930, was expelled after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Mikulov Castle, built in the Romanesque style and rebuilt in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baroque">Baroque</a> styles, houses the Regional Museum. The synagogue, originally of the 16th century, is the only preserved synagogue of the Polish type in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moravia">Moravia</a>. The Jewish cemetery, founded in the mid-15th century, has around 4,000 tombs, the oldest readable one from 1605. The town is the centre of the Mikulovská wine region.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Mikulov” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The economy rested on grain, cattle, cloth and the wine of the south around Znojmo and <b>Mikulov</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/margraviate-of-moravia">Margraviate of Moravia · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
